ontinue through the end of 2029.\n    Quote: Installing them is set to begin in the second half of 2026 and run through the end of 2029.\n- e185 [fact; QZ; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla completed the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026.\n    Quote: Tesla finished the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026, according to Reuters.\n- e186 [party_statement; QZ; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla planned to produce its AI5 chip at TSMC's Arizona plant and Samsung's Texas plant.\n    Quote: Production is planned at both TSMC 's plant in Arizona and Samsung's plant in Texas.\n- e187 [party_statement; QZ; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla targeted mid-2027 for mass production of its AI5 chip.\n    Quote: Tesla is targeting mid-2027 for mass production.\n- e188 [fact; QZ; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] SpaceX filed plans in May 2026 for a Texas chip plant called Terafab with a stated cost of $55 billion and possible total investment of up to $119 billion.\n    Quote: SpaceX, Musk's other property, filed plans in May for a $55 billion chip manufacturing plant in Texas called Terafab, Reuters reported . Total investment in the plant could reach $119 billion.\n- e189 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta 2026-07-09; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\n- e190 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Quanta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Quanta's monthly sales rose to NT$385.19 billion in June 2026, more than doubling year over year, according to Bloomberg data compiled for the item.\n    Quote: Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n- e191 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Hon Hai Precision Industry ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Hon Hai's quarterly sales rose 40% year over year, exceeding expectations, according to Bloomberg.\n    Quote: allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\n- e196 [fact; AWS; original source AWS 2026-07-10; from gnews:23d831f15e3a80d6b3ba60e6 2026-07-10] Amazon SageMaker AI introduced serverless customization for NVIDIA’s Nemotron 3 Nano and Super models.\n    Quote: Now, SageMaker AI introduces serverless model customization for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 models, starting with Nemotron 3 Nano (30B total parameters, 3B active) and Nemotron 3 Super (120B total parameters, 12B active).\n- e197 [fact; Trefis.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] NVIDIA revenue grew 71% over the last twelve months.\n    Quote: revenue grew 71% over the last twelve months\n- e198 [fact; Trefis.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] NVIDIA revenue grew 85% in the most recent quarter.\n    Quote: 85% in the most recent quarter.\n- e199 [party_statement; Trefis.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] NVIDIA management called the Blackwell architecture ramp the fastest product ramp in the company’s history.\n    Quote: the company called the “fastest product ramp in our company’s history,”\n- e201 [fact; 247wallst.com, reporting NVIDIA's figures; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year; networking revenue grew 199%.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year, and networking alone grew 199%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161, e205, e210; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e202 [party_statement; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA guided to total revenue of $91 billion for Q2 FY2027.\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance points to $91 billion in revenue.\n- e204 [fact; David Moadel, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, including Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion.\n    Quote: The company reported record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with Data Center revenue reaching $75.2 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e205 [fact; David Moadel, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA Data Center Networking revenue was $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\n    Quote: Within that segment, Data Center Networking alone hit $14.8 billion, up 199% from a year earlier, reflecting surging demand for the Spectrum-X Ethernet and NVLink interconnect systems that travel with every rack-scale Blackwell deployment.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e209 [fact; 247Wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e210 [fact; TIKR author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75 billion, up 92% year over year; the quarter is not specified.\n    Quote: Data center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Themes in force, 3]\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-10; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the current status of no completed authorization, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus or company guidance on authorization by the deadline is supplied. The specific reports that China plans to permit selected firms to buy H200s, together with the report that officials are work\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). The evidence still implies about $650 billion: the 2026 estimates and NVIDIA management's cited projection point to that level, while the power-constraint view gives no quantified reducti\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 3. That forecast implies spending just above $1 trillion, but does not establish the $1.1 trillion threshold for rung 4. The qualitative power constraint is downside pressure, but supplies no quantifie\n\n\n```\n\n### request for v14 on 2026-07-11\n```\nThesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 2 items]\n- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\n    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n- e213 [third_party_view; es.tradingview.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:1bfc0e875ab1f26dcf48fb06 2026-07-11] Morgan Stanley said AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA’s demand; it gave no period.\n    Quote: The bank noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, highlighting how enterprise AI adoption is expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\n\n[Themes in force, 3]\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The reference is the current status of no completed authorization, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus or company guidance on authorization by the deadline is supplied. The specific reports that China plans to permit selected firms to buy H200s, together with the report that officials are work\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). The evidence still implies about $650 billion: the 2026 estimates and NVIDIA management's cited projection point to that level, while the power-constraint view gives no quantified reducti\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 3. That forecast implies spending just above $1 trillion, but does not establish the $1.1 trillion threshold for rung 4. The qualitative power constraint is downside pressure, but supplies no quantifie\n\n\n```\n\n### request for v14 on 2026-07-12\n```\nThesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 28 items]\n- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\n    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n- e213 [third_party_view; es.tradingview.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:1bfc0e875ab1f26dcf48fb06 2026-07-11] Morgan Stanley said AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA’s demand; it gave no period.\n    Quote: The bank noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, highlighting how enterprise AI adoption is expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\n- e216 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated that NVIDIA held more than 90% of the AI-chip training market in 2025.\n    Quote: Nvidia 's share exceeded 90% in training in 2025.\n- e217 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated NVIDIA’s AI-chip inference market share at 60%–75% in 2025, citing competition from custom silicon.\n    Quote: Its inference share sat between 60% and 75% over the same period, undercut by growing competition from custom silicon.\n- e218 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Mizuho Securities ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Mizuho Securities estimated NVIDIA’s overall AI-chip market share at 70%–95%, depending on the segment.\n    Quote: Nvidia 's overall AI chip market share, estimated by Mizuho Securities at between 70% and 95% depending on segment\n- e219 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts said NVIDIA could lose AI-chip market share in percentage terms while increasing chip sales as the market expands.\n    Quote: Nvidia can lose share in percentage terms while selling more chips than ever.\n- e220 [fact; qz.com; original source OpenAI and Broadcom ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] OpenAI’s announced partnership with Broadcom involves co-developing custom AI chips designed to draw up to 10 gigawatts of power.\n    Quote: Its partnership with Broadcom $AVGO , announced in October 2025, involves co-developing custom AI chips that will draw up to 10 gigawatts of power, or as much electricity as 10 large nuclear reactors generate.\n- e221 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source TechCrunch ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] TechCrunch reported that 1.4 million Amazon Trainium chips across three generations were deployed as of March, including more than one million Trainium2 chips running Anthropic’s Claude.\n    Quote: As of March, 1.4 million Trainium chips were deployed across three generations, according to TechCrunch , with Anthropic's Claude running on more than one million Trainium2 chips alone.\n- e222 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Amazon committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI.\n    Quote: Amazon has also committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI, putting its chips inside two of the industry's most prominent AI labs.\n- e223 [fact; fool.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported that revenue grew 85% in its past quarter.\n    Quote: During its past quarter, Nvidia's revenue increased at an 85% clip.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e238; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e224 [third_party_view; fool.com author; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 96% in Q2.\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts expect 96% growth during Q2.\n- e229 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source Unnamed supply-chain sources, as reported by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA is reportedly cutting RTX 50-series card production by an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia is reportedly cutting production of existing RTX 50-series cards already on shelves by an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026\n- e230 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source Unnamed sources, as reported by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] The RTX 5060 was reportedly pulled from retail for at least six months.\n    Quote: The entry-level RTX 5060 has taken the hardest hit, reportedly pulled from retail entirely for at least six months .\n- e231 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source CNBC (as retold by tech-insider.org) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold all their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, with hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon buying the supply.\n    Quote: The root cause sits one layer below Nvidia entirely. Hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon are buying up high-bandwidth memory as fast as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC’s reporting from January 2026.\n- e232 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source Micron and Kearney’s PERLab, as reported by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Micron suggested memory supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027 and may return gradually to balance by 2028; Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projected the shortage could last into 2030.\n    Quote: Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 .\n- e233 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source PCWorld (as cited by tech-insider.org) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] PCWorld-cited reporting put NVIDIA AI-accelerator profit margins near 65%, versus roughly 40% for consumer gaming GPUs.\n    Quote: According to reporting cited by PCWorld, Nvidia’s AI accelerators carry profit margins near 65% , compared with roughly 40% for consumer gaming GPUs.\n- e234 [party_statement; tech-insider.org; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs during 2027–2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\n- e235 [third_party_view; jpost.com; original source jpost.com ; from gnews:c14249088492921c802c839a 2026-07-12] NVIDIA's share of the server GPU market was about 97% at the end of 2025, up from 95% at the end of 2024.\n    Quote: Nvidia's firm grip on the server GPU market showed no signs of weakening, with its market share climbing to about 97% at the end of 2025, compared to 95% the previous year.\n- e236 [third_party_view; jpost.com; original source jpost.com ; from gnews:c14249088492921c802c839a 2026-07-12] Micron's gross profit margin was 84.9% in the third quarter, versus 39% in the same quarter a year earlier; the article attributed the increase partly to high prices for high-bandwidth memory chips.\n    Quote: Micron's meteoric run was heavily influenced by high prices for high-bandwidth memory chips, which jumped the company's gross profit margin in the third quarter to 84.9%, compared to just 39% the previous year.\n- e238 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\n    Quote: For the first quarter of fiscal 2027, revenue jumped 85% year-over-year (YOY) to $81.6 billion, easily beating analyst expectations.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e223, e248; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e240 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management forecast approximately $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales.\n    Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\n- e241 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Wall Street consensus (as reported by barchart.com) ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Wall Street expected NVIDIA to generate well over $300 billion in FY2027 revenue.\n    Quote: Wall Street expects Nvidia to generate well over $300 billion in fiscal 2027 revenue, while consensus earnings estimates have moved to $8.80 per share.\n- e248 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:635f19578778394086009f6f 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in its fiscal quarter ended in late April 2026, up 85% year over year and 20% quarter over quarter.\n    Quote: In its most recent fiscal quarter (ended in late April), revenue hit a record $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% from the prior quarter.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,270p' poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Readers' packet: six sequences of two questions, NVIDIA, 2026-07-10 to 2026-07-12\n\nv9: \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"; v14: \"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report\nfor FY2027?\". Each sequence judged both questions each day it was due, from the same starting state. Apply `reading-rule.md`\n(A to D and the pass line) to each sequence. The material both questions were shown each day is at the end.\n\n## sequence-K\n- first-attempt refusals: none\n### v14 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n- reference: Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection: $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n- implied outcome: $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's projection; no separate quantified evidence in view supports adjusting it up or down.\n- reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. The supplied evidence gives no separate quantified revision to that projection: $393 billion is $7 billion below rung 2's $400 billion threshold and $43 billion above rung 4's $350 billion threshold, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\n- cites: [\"e195\"]; cites_views: []\n- why changed: This is the first judgement.\n\n### v9 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n- reference: No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied. Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-sales projection (via v14), translated using the latest reported 92% Data Center share, gives a market-reference proxy of about $362 billion, in rung 3.\n- implied outcome: About $362 billion for FY2027: $393 billion of projected total revenue multiplied by the latest reported Data Center share of 92%. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold and above the $350 billion rung-3 threshold.\n- reasons: There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance. The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share: about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The latest quarter's $75.25 billion of Data Center revenue was up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially; Q2 total-revenue guidance is $91 billion and management says Data Center revenue growth will accelerate. Those support continued growth, but do not by themselves quantify a higher full-year segment forecast. Management's roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin visibility spans two calendar years and products, so it is not treated as FY2027 Data Center revenue in full. Competition and memory-supply constraints temper the upside. The implied $362 billion is below the $400 billion boundary for rung 2 and above the $350 billion boundary for rung 3, and it remains below the $393 billion total-revenue forecast.\n- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e109\", \"e116\", \"e117\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e163\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n- why changed: The rung changes from 2 to 3. The prior $420 billion estimate extrapolated Q1 sequential growth; this judgment instead anchors to the current $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection and applies the reported 92% Data Center mix, implying about $362 billion.\n\n### v14 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n- reference: Bloomberg’s FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg’s stated projection; the separate 20% AI-laboratory demand figure has no period or revenue amount from which to derive a revision.\n- reasons: Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 projection is in rung 3, $7 billion below rung 2’s $400 billion threshold. The newer Morgan Stanley demand-mix figure adds no period or revenue amount, so it does not quantify a revision across that boundary. The bullish capex and China-authorization themes are consistent with upside risk, but do not size an increase enough to move the forecast from Bloomberg’s projection.\n- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\"]; cites_views: []\n- why changed: The rung and implied revenue remain unchanged, but the newly supplied Morgan Stanley demand-mix estimate adds a relevant supporting fact: AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA demand. It has no period or revenue quantification, so it does not change the implied FY2027 figure.\n\n### v9 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, translated at the latest reported 92% Data Center share to about $362 billion; this sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\n- implied outcome: About $356 billion: Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion; applying the 92% latest segment share to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide implies about $83.7 billion for Q2. Carrying forward the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by the Q2 total-revenue guide gives about $93.3 billion in Q3 and $104 billion in Q4, for about $356 billion in FY2027.\n- reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The current run-rate estimate is about $356 billion, also rung 3: the implied quarterly progression does not reach rung 2's $400 billion floor. Q1's reported result and Q2 guidance support a strong trajectory, while the stated acceleration and multi-year visibility add upside but do not quantify enough FY2027 segment revenue to move the forecast across a boundary. Competition and memory constraints temper that upside. The implied segment figure remains below the dependent thesis's $393 billion total-revenue forecast.\n- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e109\", \"