{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md | sed -n '88,175p'\"","aggregated_output":"    88\t## sequence-M\n    89\t- first-attempt refusals: none\n    90\t### v14 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n    91\t- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3.\n    92\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion in FY2027, using Bloomberg's specific projected sales figure. No supplied quantified adjustment supports carrying that estimate across the $400 billion boundary.\n    93\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). That specific forecast implies $393 billion, $7 billion below the rung 2 threshold. The favorable China authorization and hyperscaler capex themes push upward, but supply no quantified NVIDIA revenue increment that would move the implied forecast across $400 billion.\n    94\t- cites: [\"e195\"]; cites_views: []\n    95\t- why changed: First judgement; changed is true as required.\n    96\t\n    97\t### v9 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n    98\t- reference: There is no supplied direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance. The available market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection (v14, rung 3); using the reported Q1 Data Center share of about 92% gives an indicative Data Center reference of about $362 billion, rung 3.\n    99\t- implied outcome: Approximately $356 billion for FY2027: apply the reported 92% Data Center share to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide to estimate Q2 Data Center revenue at about $83.7 billion, then carry the guide-implied 11.5% sequential total-revenue growth rate forward for Q3 and Q4, adding the $75.25 billion Q1 actual.\n   100\t- reasons: The available market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, which v14 places in rung 3; applying the latest reported Data Center share of about 92% gives an indicative Data Center reference of about $362 billion, also rung 3. The forecast is about $356 billion: the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide implies roughly $83.7 billion of Data Center revenue if the Q1 92% share holds, and carrying the guide's 11.5% sequential total-revenue growth rate forward for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $75.25B + $83.7B + $93.3B + $104.0B. This is below the $400 billion boundary and consistent with Data Center being a portion of v14's $393 billion total-revenue forecast; management's unquantified Q2 growth-acceleration statement and the $1 trillion visibility figure spanning two calendar years do not supply a quantified basis to move it above $400 billion. The new Q2 guide changes the key run-rate input from the previous approximately $420 billion estimate and moves the forecast from rung 2 to rung 3.\n   101\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e204\", \"e205\", \"e116\", \"e117\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e159\", \"e190\", \"e191\", \"e201\", \"e209\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n   102\t- why changed: The forecast changes from rung 2 and approximately $420 billion to rung 3 and approximately $356 billion. The new quantified Q2 total-revenue guide supplies a slower sequential run-rate input than the prior extrapolation; the repeated Q1 revenue reports do not themselves add a new fact.\n   103\t\n   104\t### v14 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n   105\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   106\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's projection. The AI-laboratory demand share has no stated period or revenue amount, so it does not support a quantified adjustment.\n   107\t- reasons: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection is $7 billion below the $400 billion threshold for rung 2. The reported 20% demand share from AI laboratories is a positive demand signal, but it gives no period or revenue increment, so it does not move the implied figure across a rung boundary.\n   108\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\"]; cites_views: []\n   109\t- why changed: The rung and implied revenue remain unchanged, but e213 adds a new, specific demand-mix fact that modestly supports the forecast; it does not quantify an FY2027 revenue adjustment.\n   110\t\n   111\t### v9 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n   112\t- reference: Bloomberg's approximately $393 billion FY2027 NVIDIA total-revenue projection is the market reference. At the latest reported 92% Data Center share, that corresponds to about $362 billion, in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\n   113\t- implied outcome: About $356 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1, approximately $83.7 billion in Q2 using the $91 billion total-revenue guide and a 92% Data Center share, then approximately $93.4 billion and $104.1 billion in Q3 and Q4 by carrying forward the 11.5% sequential growth implied by the Q2 total-revenue guide.\n   114\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's approximately $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest reported 92% Data Center share implies about $362 billion for Data Center, which is rung 3. The Q2 total-revenue guide implies 11.5% sequential growth from Q1. Using the Q1 Data Center share as a proxy, then carrying that guided growth rate through the remaining quarters, implies roughly $356 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. The acceleration statement and the $1 trillion visibility figure are upside evidence, but neither supplies a FY2027 Data Center amount: the visibility spans 2026–27 and multiple products, so it cannot be assigned in full here. New hyperscaler tracking and supplier sales provide further positive signals, while Chinese substitution and memory constraints are counterweights; none quantifies an adjustment large enough to cross $400 billion. The implied segment forecast is also below v14's $393 billion total-company forecast, as required.\n   115\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e202\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e162\", \"e163\", \"e190\", \"e191\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e159\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n   116\t- why changed: The rung and the run-rate estimate remain at rung 3 and about $356 billion, but newer Bank of America hyperscaler-sales tracking and supplier-sales reports add bullish demand and supply-chain facts; newer evidence on Chinese substitution and memory constraints adds countervailing pressure. These change the supporting evidence mix without quantifying a different FY2027 estimate.\n   117\t\n   118\t### v14 on 2026-07-12: rung 2, changed True\n   119\t- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the most specific market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   120\t- implied outcome: Approximately $400 billion: Bloomberg's $393 billion projection assumes 82% annual growth, while Q1 revenue grew 85% year over year and management guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion. Carrying that stronger near-term growth pace forward, while allowing for later-period moderation and supply/competition risks, puts the forecast around the $400 billion threshold.\n   121\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The latest quarter's 85% year-over-year growth and management's $91 billion Q2 guide support a near-term growth pace at least as strong as Bloomberg's projected 82% full-year growth. That lifts the implied outcome to around $400 billion, just across the rung 2 threshold. Demand from hyperscalers and the AI-capex outlook support the upside, while memory constraints, domestic Chinese alternatives, and custom-chip deployments temper it.\n   122\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e220\", \"e221\", \"e222\", \"e231\", \"e232\", \"e238\", \"e240\", \"e241\", \"e252\", \"e253\", \"e257\"]; cites_views: []\n   123\t- why changed: The implied FY2027 outcome has moved from Bloomberg's $393 billion projection to approximately $400 billion after the reported Q1 result and Q2 guidance showed near-term growth at or above the pace embedded in that annual projection. This crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2.\n   124\t\n   125\t### v9 on 2026-07-12: rung 3, changed False\n   126\t- reference: Bloomberg's approximately $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using the latest 92% Data Center share, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue; this reference is in rung 3.\n   127\t- implied outcome: About $356 billion: use Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, estimate Q2 at approximately 92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide ($83.7 billion), then carry the guide's 11.5% sequential growth rate forward for Q3 and Q4 ($93.3 billion and $104.0 billion).\n   128\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's approximately $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center share gives roughly $362 billion, which sits in rung 3. Independently, Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion and management guided Q2 total revenue to about $91 billion. Using the Q1 segment share as a proxy gives about $83.7 billion of Q2 Data Center revenue; carrying the 11.5% sequential total-revenue guide forward for Q3 and Q4 implies about $93.3 billion and $104.0 billion, respectively, or roughly $356 billion for FY2027. Management's statement that Data Center growth will accelerate in Q2 is upside, but does not quantify a higher figure; the broader $1 trillion visibility spans 2026–27 and multiple products, so it is discounted rather than assigned in full. Competition, China substitution, and memory constraints temper the forecast. The estimate is consistent with v14's approximately $400 billion total-revenue forecast: this segment estimate is below the whole and implies about an 89% share. The implied figure remains within rung 3's $350 billion to below $400 billion range.\n   129\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e255\", \"e171\", \"e162\", \"e163\", \"e109\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e158\", \"e159\", \"e116\", \"e117\", \"e227\", \"e190\", \"e191\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n   130\t- why changed: The rung and the key implied figure are unchanged from the previous conclusion: the forecast remains about $356 billion, within rung 3. The current evidence does not quantify a segment-level revision sufficient to change that estimate.\n   131\t\n   132\t## sequence-N\n   133\t- first-attempt refusals: none\n   134\t### v14 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n   135\t- reference: Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   136\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's explicit projection; it is up 82% year over year and remains below the $400 billion threshold for rung 2.\n   137\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The supplied evidence gives no quantified revision or other revenue input that changes that projection; the implied outcome remains $393 billion, below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold and above the $350 billion rung-3 floor. The bullish China authorization and hyperscaler capex themes support upside directionally, but provide no NVIDIA revenue amount that would justify crossing a boundary.\n   138\t- cites: [\"e195\"]; cites_views: []\n   139\t- why changed: This is the first judgement.\n   140\t\n   141\t### v9 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n   142\t- reference: Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion in v14; this is not a direct Data Center consensus. At the reported Q1 Data Center share of about 92%, the reference implies approximately $362 billion for Data Center, which is rung 3.\n   143\t- implied outcome: Approximately $370 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: start with the roughly $362 billion proxy from 92% of v14's $393 billion total-revenue forecast and allow a modest uplift for management's Q2 Data Center growth acceleration. The result remains below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold and below the $393 billion total-revenue whole.\n   144\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion in dependency v14. It is not a direct Data Center consensus; applying the reported Q1 Data Center share of about 92% gives a segment proxy of roughly $362 billion, in rung 3. Q1 growth and management's Q2 acceleration guidance support a strong run-rate, but the Q2 total-revenue guide is not a segment figure, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility spans products and two calendar years. The implied Data Center outcome is about $370 billion: modestly above the 92%-share proxy for acceleration, but below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. It is also consistent with v14's $393 billion forecast for total revenue, which caps the segment as a part of that whole.\n   145\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e153\", \"e116\", \"e117\", \"e109\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n   146\t- why changed: The prior rung-2 forecast was about $420 billion and lacked a direct market reference. The current judgment uses v14's $393 billion total-revenue reference, yielding a roughly $362 billion segment proxy at the observed 92% mix, and respects the total-revenue dependency ceiling; the implied outcome is now below $400 billion.\n   147\t\n   148\t### v14 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n   149\t- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it falls in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   150\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's stated projection. The additional demand-composition view and the themes provide no quantified NVIDIA revenue adjustment.\n   151\t- reasons: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3. The new Morgan Stanley view that AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA demand, with enterprise adoption expanding, is directionally supportive but gives no period or revenue amount to add to that projection. The implied outcome therefore remains $393 billion: $7 billion below rung 2's $400 billion threshold and above rung 3's $350 billion floor.\n   152\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\"]; cites_views: []\n   153\t- why changed: The rung and implied figure are unchanged, but e213 adds a new, quantified fact about demand composition and a positive enterprise-adoption view. It supports the outlook directionally without supplying a period or revenue estimate that changes the implied $393 billion.\n   154\t\n   155\t### v9 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n   156\t- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, as stated in v14, is the available market reference. It is not a direct Data Center estimate; applying the Q1 segment's roughly 92% share gives a proxy of about $362 billion, which is rung 3.\n   157\t- implied outcome: About $370 billion for FY2027: start from the roughly $362 billion segment proxy implied by v14's $393 billion total-revenue projection and the Q1 Data Center share, then allow a modest uplift for Q1's 21% sequential segment growth, the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide, and management's Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration statement. The estimate remains below the $393 billion total-revenue forecast.\n   158\t- reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection in dependency v14. It is not a direct Data Center consensus; applying the reported Q1 Data Center share of about 92% puts the segment proxy at roughly $362 billion, in rung 3. The Q1 result and 21% sequential growth, the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide, and management's statement that Data Center revenue growth would accelerate in Q2 support a modest uplift from that proxy. The implied roughly $370 billion remains below rung 2's $400 billion boundary and is consistent with the $393 billion forecast for total revenue, since Data Center is only part of the total. Demand and capacity evidence is mixed: hyperscaler sales and capex support growth, while competition, memory tightness, and China substitution temper it.\n   159\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e109\", \"e116\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e162\", \"e163\", \"e159\", \"e190\", \"e191\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n   160\t- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the current assessment incorporates the stated $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide and the latest reported Q1 Data Center figure and sequential growth in deriving the implied outcome. Those updated quantitative inputs change the supporting facts even though the estimate remains about $370 billion.\n   161\t\n   162\t### v14 on 2026-07-12: rung 3, changed True\n   163\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   164\t- implied outcome: Starting with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth. Carrying that growth forward gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion total for FY2027.\n   165\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. NVIDIA reported $81.6 billion in Q1 and guided to $91 billion for Q2; carrying the implied 11.5% sequential growth from Q1 to Q2 forward gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below rung 2's $400 billion boundary and above rung 3's $350 billion floor. The sequential run-rate is tempered by reported HBM tightness and China substitution pressure, while AI demand, capacity spending, and the authorization theme provide offsets; none of the quantified evidence moves the implied total across a rung boundary.\n   166\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e238\", \"e240\", \"e231\", \"e232\", \"e252\", \"e253\", \"e220\", \"e221\", \"e222\", \"e234\", \"e257\"]; cites_views: []\n   167\t- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the implied outcome changes from the prior $393 billion reference-based estimate to about $387 billion after incorporating Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance and carrying the sequential growth rate forward.\n   168\t\n   169\t### v9 on 2026-07-12: rung 3, changed True\n   170\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, converted using NVIDIA's roughly 92% Q1 Data Center share, implies about $362 billion for Data Center and sits in rung 3.\n   171\t- implied outcome: About $356 billion from 92% of the current v14 forecast of roughly $387 billion total revenue; modest upside for the Q2 Data Center growth outlook supports an estimate around $360 billion.\n   172\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the reported Q1 Data Center share of about 92% gives a segment proxy of roughly $362 billion, which sits in rung 3. The current dependency forecast of about $387 billion in total FY2027 revenue implies roughly $356 billion of Data Center revenue at that same share; allowing modest upside for management's Q2 Data Center growth outlook supports an estimate around $360 billion. Q1 growth and hyperscaler demand support the forecast, while competition, memory constraints and China substitution temper it. This remains below rung 2's $400 billion boundary, and the segment estimate does not exceed the dependency's total-revenue forecast.\n   173\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e255\", \"e109\", \"e116\", \"e153\", \"e162\", \"e163\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n   174\t- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the supporting implied outcome changes from roughly $370 billion to about $360 billion, using the current v14 total-revenue forecast rather than the prior estimate.\n   175\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
