{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md | sed -n '176,268p;448,542p'\"","aggregated_output":"   176\t## sequence-P\n   177\t- first-attempt refusals: none\n   178\t### v14 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n   179\t- reference: Bloomberg-compiled projections put FY2027 sales at $393 billion; that reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   180\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's stated full-year projection; it is $7 billion short of rung 2's $400 billion lower boundary.\n   181\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains below rung 2's $400 billion threshold by $7 billion. The in-force China authorization and hyperscaler capex conclusions support upside, but do not quantify an NVIDIA revenue uplift sufficient to move the forecast across that boundary.\n   182\t- cites: [\"e195\"]; cites_views: []\n   183\t- why changed: First judgement.\n   184\t\n   185\t### v9 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n   186\t- reference: No direct FY2027 Data Center market consensus or company segment guidance is supplied. NVIDIA's roughly $1 trillion Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026 and 2027 equates to about $500 billion annually if evenly split, which maps to rung 1, but is a broad multi-product, multi-period reference rather than a FY2027 segment forecast. The $91 billion Q2 guide is total company revenue and does not itself set a direct segment rung.\n   187\t- implied outcome: About $365–380 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. Q1 reported $75.25 billion; applying its 92% segment share to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide gives an approximately $84 billion Q2 proxy, adjusted upward modestly for management's unquantified acceleration guidance, then carrying roughly 12–15% sequential growth through the remaining quarters.\n   188\t- reasons: There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance in the supplied evidence. The closest company reference is roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026 and 2027; an even split implies about $500 billion per year, in rung 1, but it spans products and periods and is not a FY2027 Data Center forecast. The $91 billion Q2 FY2027 guide is for total company revenue, not the segment. The implied FY2027 Data Center outcome is about $365–380 billion: using Q1's 92% Data Center share as a rough mix proxy gives about $84 billion for Q2, with some upside allowed for management's unquantified Q2 acceleration, then carrying roughly 12–15% sequential growth through the remaining quarters. That remains below $400 billion and in rung 3; the broad visibility figure supports upside but does not outweigh the near-term total-revenue constraint enough to cross the boundary.\n   189\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e116\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e158\", \"e163\", \"e159\"]; cites_views: []\n   190\t- why changed: The forecast moves from rung 2 to rung 3 because the supplied $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide puts a tighter near-term constraint on the segment run-rate than the prior projection based mainly on Q1's 21% sequential growth. The implied FY2027 estimate is now about $365–380 billion; the unquantified acceleration and multi-period revenue visibility remain supportive but do not restore the estimate to $400 billion or more.\n   191\t\n   192\t### v14 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n   193\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   194\t- implied outcome: The evidence implies roughly $393 billion, with unquantified upside pressure from AI-lab demand and the in-force China and hyperscaler-capex conclusions. No supplied figure supports adding the $7 billion needed to reach $400 billion.\n   195\t- reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 and is $7 billion below rung 2. The AI-lab demand figure adds a positive demand-mix signal, and the in-force China and hyperscaler-capex conclusions also lean bullish; however, none supplies a quantified FY2027 NVIDIA revenue increase. The evidence therefore still implies revenue below $400 billion, without crossing a rung boundary.\n   196\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\"]; cites_views: []\n   197\t- why changed: The rung is unchanged, but e213 adds a new supporting fact: AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA demand, with no period or quantified revenue impact. This updates the facts supporting the forecast but does not justify moving the implied figure across $400 billion.\n   198\t\n   199\t### v14 on 2026-07-12: rung 2, changed True\n   200\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection (e195) is the reference and sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\n   201\t- implied outcome: About $411 billion: Bloomberg's $393 billion projection at 82% growth implies a FY2026 base of about $216 billion; carrying forward the roughly 90.5% average of Q1's reported 85% growth and the Q2 96% growth expectation gives approximately $411 billion. Management's $91 billion Q2 revenue guidance supports the near-term run-rate.\n   202\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The new Q1 result and Q2 outlook imply a faster growth run-rate: applying the roughly 90.5% average of Q1's reported 85% growth and the 96% Q2 growth expectation to the approximately $216 billion FY2026 base implied by Bloomberg's projection gives about $411 billion, in rung 2. The $91 billion management Q2 guidance supports the stronger near-term trajectory, while supply constraints and competition are downside risks; they do not outweigh the run-rate shift below $400 billion.\n   203\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\", \"e216\", \"e217\", \"e218\", \"e219\", \"e220\", \"e221\", \"e222\", \"e223\", \"e224\", \"e229\", \"e230\", \"e231\", \"e232\", \"e234\", \"e235\", \"e238\", \"e240\", \"e241\", \"e248\", \"e252\", \"e253\", \"e254\", \"e255\", \"e257\"]; cites_views: []\n   204\t- why changed: The rung moves from 3 to 2 because the reported Q1 growth and Q2 outlook imply a FY2027 run-rate above the $400 billion boundary; this also changes the implied figure from below $400 billion to about $411 billion.\n   205\t\n   206\t### v9 on 2026-07-12: rung 3, changed True\n   207\t- reference: No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied. Management's roughly $1 trillion Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026 and 2027 is the closest plan reference; an even split implies $500 billion annually, which sits in rung 1, but it spans products and periods. The $91 billion Q2 FY2027 guidance is total-company revenue, not a rung-level annual Data Center reference.\n   208\t- implied outcome: About $390–395 billion for FY2027 Data Center: Q1 was $75.25 billion, and applying the 92% Q1 segment mix to the $91 billion Q2 total guide gives roughly $84 billion for Q2, with some allowance for management's stated Q2 acceleration. Carrying strong sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, with the multi-year product visibility as upside but discounted because it is not FY2027 segment revenue, leaves the central estimate below $400 billion.\n   209\t- reasons: There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance. The strongest company reference is roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026 and 2027; an even split is $500 billion per year, in rung 1, but that is a multi-product, multi-period visibility figure rather than a FY2027 Data Center forecast. The $91 billion Q2 guide is total-company revenue and does not itself set an annual segment rung. Against those references, the Q1 Data Center run-rate, Q2 total-revenue guide, and stated Q2 acceleration support an outcome around $390–395 billion. Strong demand and the product visibility add upside, but not enough to make $400 billion the most likely result, so the estimate remains in rung 3.\n   210\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e255\", \"e162\", \"e163\", \"e116\", \"e159\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e158\", \"e227\", \"e177\"]; cites_views: []\n   211\t- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the supporting implied outcome moves up from the previous $365–380 billion estimate: Q1 Data Center revenue was up 21% sequentially, management guided to Q2 acceleration, and the $1 trillion product-visibility figure adds upside, though it is not a direct FY2027 segment forecast.\n   212\t\n   213\t## sequence-Q\n   214\t- first-attempt refusals: none\n   215\t### v14 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n   216\t- reference: Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   217\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion in FY2027 revenue, using Bloomberg's quantified projection directly. That lies below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold and above the $350 billion rung-3 floor.\n   218\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion, which falls in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The implied outcome remains in rung 3: the in-force quantified projection is $393 billion, while the in-force themes provide upside pressure but no quantified revenue revision sufficient to imply at least $400 billion.\n   219\t- cites: [\"e195\"]; cites_views: []\n   220\t- why changed: This is the first judgement.\n   221\t\n   222\t### v9 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n   223\t- reference: No direct FY2027 Data Center revenue consensus or company guidance is supplied, so no reference rung can be assigned. The explicit $91 billion Q2 FY2027 company guide is for total revenue, not FY2027 Data Center revenue, and does not itself map to a ladder rung.\n   224\t- implied outcome: About $356 billion mechanically, rounded to a central estimate of about $360 billion: applying the Q1 Data Center mix of 92% to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide implies roughly $84 billion of Q2 Data Center revenue; carrying forward the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by the total-revenue guide for Q3 and Q4 gives about $356 billion across FY2027. Management’s acceleration comment and multiyear visibility justify modest support around that run-rate, not enough to imply $400 billion.\n   225\t- reasons: The FY2027 Data Center-specific market consensus and company guidance are not supplied, so there is no direct reference rung. The new Q2 total-revenue guide implies a slower sequential carry-forward than the Q1 Data Center segment’s 21% sequential growth: using its 92% Q1 revenue mix as a rough Q2 estimate and carrying forward the Q2 guided total-revenue growth rate gives about $356 billion for FY2027. Management’s acceleration comment and multiyear visibility support some upside, but are offset by memory constraints, competition and the fact that the visibility spans products and calendar years. The central estimate is about $360 billion, within rung 3 and below the $400 billion boundary for rung 2.\n   226\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e116\", \"e117\", \"e163\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e158\", \"e177\", \"e179\", \"e180\", \"e184\"]; cites_views: []\n   227\t- why changed: The rung changes from 2 to 3 because the Q2 total-revenue guidance now supplies a more concrete, slower growth rate to carry forward than the prior estimate’s repeated 21% sequential Data Center growth. This also changes the implied FY2027 estimate from about $420 billion to about $360 billion.\n   228\t\n   229\t### v14 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n   230\t- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n   231\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's explicit projection. The reported 82% growth and the qualitative demand-share comment do not provide a quantified revision that would lift the estimate to $400 billion.\n   232\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3. That figure is $7 billion below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The additional report that AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA demand is a positive demand signal, but gives no period or revenue change to apply; the in-force capex and export-control themes also lean upward without quantifying a revenue revision. The evidence therefore does not imply a figure crossing the boundary.\n   233\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\"]; cites_views: []\n   234\t- why changed: The rung and implied FY2027 figure remain the same, but the newly supplied report adds a specific demand-composition fact: AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA demand. It does not quantify an FY2027 revenue revision.\n   235\t\n   236\t### v14 on 2026-07-12: rung 2, changed True\n   237\t- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion (e195) is the clearest market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The broader statement that Wall Street expects well over $300 billion (e241) does not specify a more precise rung.\n   238\t- implied outcome: Bloomberg's $393 billion at 82% growth implies an FY2026 base of about $216 billion. Applying an approximately 90.5% growth run-rate—the average of reported Q1 growth of 85% and analysts' Q2 growth expectation of 96%, supported by management's $91 billion Q2 guide—implies roughly $411 billion for FY2027.\n   239\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3 and is $7 billion short of rung 2. The latest reported 85% Q1 growth and the 96% Q2 growth expected by analysts, alongside management's $91 billion Q2 guidance, imply a faster growth run-rate than Bloomberg's 82% full-year growth assumption. Carrying forward roughly the average of the Q1 and Q2 growth rates puts the implied result around $411 billion, above the $400 billion boundary; this is a forecast, not a claim that the full-year outcome is already established. Supply constraints and competition are downside offsets, but the evidence shown does not quantify them enough to pull the estimate below $400 billion.\n   240\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e238\", \"e240\", \"e224\", \"e231\", \"e232\", \"e234\", \"e252\", \"e253\", \"e257\"]; cites_views: []\n   241\t- why changed: The latest Q1 result and Q2 outlook shift the implied FY2027 growth rate above Bloomberg's 82% assumption, moving the estimate from about $393 billion to roughly $411 billion and across the $400 billion rung boundary.\n   242\t\n   243\t### v9 on 2026-07-12: rung 3, changed True\n   244\t- reference: No FY2027 Data Center-specific consensus or guidance is supplied. The nearest reference proxy is the Q2 FY2027 total-revenue guide of approximately $91 billion; applying the Q1 Data Center mix and carrying the guide-implied growth forward gives about $356 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3.\n   245\t- implied outcome: About $370 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. The guide-and-mix carry-forward baseline is roughly $356 billion; I raise it modestly for the Q2 acceleration comment, multiyear processor revenue visibility, and quantified evidence of hyperscaler wallet-share gains, while discounting that visibility because it spans products and calendar years.\n   246\t- reasons: No FY2027 Data Center-specific market consensus or company guidance is supplied. As a reference proxy, the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide, applied to the Q1 Data Center mix of about 92% and carried forward at the guide-implied sequential growth rate, points to roughly $356 billion for FY2027—rung 3. The implied outcome is modestly higher, about $370 billion: the Q2 acceleration comment, $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026 and 2027, and evidence of hyperscaler wallet-share gains support upside, but that visibility spans products and years and is not all FY2027 Data Center revenue. The estimate remains below rung 2’s $400 billion boundary; memory constraints and custom-chip competition limit the upward adjustment.\n   247\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e255\", \"e162\", \"e163\", \"e116\", \"e117\", \"e246\", \"e247\", \"e109\", \"e227\"]; cites_views: []\n   248\t- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the implied outcome and supporting facts change modestly from the previous roughly $360 billion estimate: the quantified hyperscaler-sales and wallet-share evidence (e162, e163) strengthens the demand outlook, while the reported memory partnership (e246, e247) provides some supply support. These inputs raise the central estimate to about $370 billion, still short of $400 billion.\n   249\t\n   250\t## The material shown (control-1's requests, previous conclusion removed)\n   251\t\n   252\t### request for v14 on 2026-07-10\n   253\t```\n   254\tThesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n   255\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\n   256\t\n   257\t[Evidence in view under this thesis, 1 items]\n   258\t- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews: