company guidance on authorization by the deadline is supplied. The specific reports that China plans to permit selected firms to buy H200s, together with the report that officials are work\n   461\t- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). The evidence still implies about $650 billion: the 2026 estimates and NVIDIA management's cited projection point to that level, while the power-constraint view gives no quantified reducti\n   462\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 3. That forecast implies spending just above $1 trillion, but does not establish the $1.1 trillion threshold for rung 4. The qualitative power constraint is downside pressure, but supplies no quantifie\n   463\t\n   464\t\n   465\t```\n   466\t\n   467\t### request for v14 on 2026-07-12\n   468\t```\n   469\tThesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n   470\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\n   471\t\n   472\t[Evidence in view under this thesis, 28 items]\n   473\t- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\n   474\t    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n   475\t- e213 [third_party_view; es.tradingview.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:1bfc0e875ab1f26dcf48fb06 2026-07-11] Morgan Stanley said AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA’s demand; it gave no period.\n   476\t    Quote: The bank noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, highlighting how enterprise AI adoption is expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\n   477\t- e216 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated that NVIDIA held more than 90% of the AI-chip training market in 2025.\n   478\t    Quote: Nvidia 's share exceeded 90% in training in 2025.\n   479\t- e217 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated NVIDIA’s AI-chip inference market share at 60%–75% in 2025, citing competition from custom silicon.\n   480\t    Quote: Its inference share sat between 60% and 75% over the same period, undercut by growing competition from custom silicon.\n   481\t- e218 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Mizuho Securities ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Mizuho Securities estimated NVIDIA’s overall AI-chip market share at 70%–95%, depending on the segment.\n   482\t    Quote: Nvidia 's overall AI chip market share, estimated by Mizuho Securities at between 70% and 95% depending on segment\n   483\t- e219 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts said NVIDIA could lose AI-chip market share in percentage terms while increasing chip sales as the market expands.\n   484\t    Quote: Nvidia can lose share in percentage terms while selling more chips than ever.\n   485\t- e220 [fact; qz.com; original source OpenAI and Broadcom ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] OpenAI’s announced partnership with Broadcom involves co-developing custom AI chips designed to draw up to 10 gigawatts of power.\n   486\t    Quote: Its partnership with Broadcom $AVGO , announced in October 2025, involves co-developing custom AI chips that will draw up to 10 gigawatts of power, or as much electricity as 10 large nuclear reactors generate.\n   487\t- e221 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source TechCrunch ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] TechCrunch reported that 1.4 million Amazon Trainium chips across three generations were deployed as of March, including more than one million Trainium2 chips running Anthropic’s Claude.\n   488\t    Quote: As of March, 1.4 million Trainium chips were deployed across three generations, according to TechCrunch , with Anthropic's Claude running on more than one million Trainium2 chips alone.\n   489\t- e222 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Amazon committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI.\n   490\t    Quote: Amazon has also committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI, putting its chips inside two of the industry's most prominent AI labs.\n   491\t- e223 [fact; fool.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported that revenue grew 85% in its past quarter.\n   492\t    Quote: During its past quarter, Nvidia's revenue increased at an 85% clip.\n   493\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e238; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   494\t- e224 [third_party_view; fool.com author; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 96% in Q2.\n   495\t    Quote: Wall Street analysts expect 96% growth during Q2.\n   496\t- e229 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source Unnamed supply-chain sources, as reported by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA is reportedly cutting RTX 50-series card production by an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026.\n   497\t    Quote: Nvidia is reportedly cutting production of existing RTX 50-series cards already on shelves by an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026\n   498\t- e230 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source Unnamed sources, as reported by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] The RTX 5060 was reportedly pulled from retail for at least six months.\n   499\t    Quote: The entry-level RTX 5060 has taken the hardest hit, reportedly pulled from retail entirely for at least six months .\n   500\t- e231 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source CNBC (as retold by tech-insider.org) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold all their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, with hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon buying the supply.\n   501\t    Quote: The root cause sits one layer below Nvidia entirely. Hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon are buying up high-bandwidth memory as fast as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC’s reporting from January 2026.\n   502\t- e232 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source Micron and Kearney’s PERLab, as reported by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Micron suggested memory supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027 and may return gradually to balance by 2028; Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projected the shortage could last into 2030.\n   503\t    Quote: Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 .\n   504\t- e233 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source PCWorld (as cited by tech-insider.org) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] PCWorld-cited reporting put NVIDIA AI-accelerator profit margins near 65%, versus roughly 40% for consumer gaming GPUs.\n   505\t    Quote: According to reporting cited by PCWorld, Nvidia’s AI accelerators carry profit margins near 65% , compared with roughly 40% for consumer gaming GPUs.\n   506\t- e234 [party_statement; tech-insider.org; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs during 2027–2028.\n   507\t    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\n   508\t- e235 [third_party_view; jpost.com; original source jpost.com ; from gnews:c14249088492921c802c839a 2026-07-12] NVIDIA's share of the server GPU market was about 97% at the end of 2025, up from 95% at the end of 2024.\n   509\t    Quote: Nvidia's firm grip on the server GPU market showed no signs of weakening, with its market share climbing to about 97% at the end of 2025, compared to 95% the previous year.\n   510\t- e236 [third_party_view; jpost.com; original source jpost.com ; from gnews:c14249088492921c802c839a 2026-07-12] Micron's gross profit margin was 84.9% in the third quarter, versus 39% in the same quarter a year earlier; the article attributed the increase partly to high prices for high-bandwidth memory chips.\n   511\t    Quote: Micron's meteoric run was heavily influenced by high prices for high-bandwidth memory chips, which jumped the company's gross profit margin in the third quarter to 84.9%, compared to just 39% the previous year.\n   512\t- e238 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\n   513\t    Quote: For the first quarter of fiscal 2027, revenue jumped 85% year-over-year (YOY) to $81.6 billion, easily beating analyst expectations.\n   514\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e223, e248; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   515\t- e240 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management forecast approximately $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales.\n   516\t    Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\n   517\t- e241 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Wall Street consensus (as reported by barchart.com) ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Wall Street expected NVIDIA to generate well over $300 billion in FY2027 revenue.\n   518\t    Quote: Wall Street expects Nvidia to generate well over $300 billion in fiscal 2027 revenue, while consensus earnings estimates have moved to $8.80 per share.\n   519\t- e248 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:635f19578778394086009f6f 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in its fiscal quarter ended in late April 2026, up 85% year over year and 20% quarter over quarter.\n   520\t    Quote: In its most recent fiscal quarter (ended in late April), revenue hit a record $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% from the prior quarter.\n   521\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e238; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   522\t- e252 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Chinese technology companies surveyed by Bloomberg ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] A Bloomberg survey reported that Chinese technology companies expect to allocate about 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\n   523\t    Quote: A new Bloomberg survey showed Chinese technology companies expect to allocate roughly 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\n   524\t- e253 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Beijing ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The author said Beijing had encouraged companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, adding pressure to NVIDIA’s growth in China.\n   525\t    Quote: Beijing has also encouraged companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, creating additional pressure on Nvidia's long-term growth in one of its largest overseas markets.\n   526\t- e254 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported record FY2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\n   527\t    Quote: During the fiscal first quarter of 2027, Nvidia generated record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year-over-year (YOY).\n   528\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   529\t- e255 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\n   530\t    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\n   531\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   532\t- e257 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The author reported that NVIDIA had recently expanded partnerships with Alphabet and Marvell.\n   533\t    Quote: expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL),\n   534\t\n   535\t[Themes in force, 3]\n   536\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The reference is the current status of no completed authorization, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus or company guidance on authorization by the deadline is supplied. The specific reports that China plans to permit selected firms to buy H200s, together with the report that officials are work\n   537\t- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). The evidence still implies about $650 billion: the 2026 estimates and NVIDIA management's cited projection point to that level, while the power-constraint view gives no quantified reducti\n   538\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-12; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies spending around, and modestly above, $1 trillion: it does not quantify a move to the $1.1 trillion boundary, while the stated power constraint is downside pressure withou\n   539\t\n   540\t\n   541\t```\n   542\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
