{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '269,447p' poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md\"","aggregated_output":"### request for v9 on 2026-07-10\n```\nThesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 72 items]\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e104 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA and Nokia 2026-07-07; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to integrate Nokia anyRAN software with NVIDIA AI-RAN platforms for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\n- e105 [fact; NVIDIA; original source SoftBank and NVIDIA ; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] SoftBank and NVIDIA reported a trial of stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform, delivering roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\n    Quote: SoftBank and NVIDIA recently reported stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform. The trial delivered roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\n- e106 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Palantir Technologies ; from gnews:1a116fecd5de1747cc5a06b1 2026-07-07] Palantir announced it is combining its new intelligence engine with NVIDIA’s Nemotron open AI models to deliver mission-focused AI for U.S. government agencies and major infrastructure operators.\n    Quote: Earlier this week, Palantir announced that it is combining its new intelligence engine and Nvidia's Nemotron open AI models to deliver mission-focused AI for government agencies and major infrastructure operators in the U.S.\n- e108 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its profit-sharing plan reflects a shift in AI demand from model building toward production inference that requires infrastructure to run continuously at scale.\n    Quote: Nvidia said that this plan highlights a transition in AI demand from model building towards production inference tasks, needing continuous running of infrastructure at scale.\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA’s AI computing products.\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\n- e114 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier.\n    Quote: That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\n- e116 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\n    Quote: tight market conditions could last until 2028\n- e117 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author said new capacity for many companies in the memory-chip industry would not be online until 2027 or later.\n    Quote: new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later\n- e122 [fact; 247Wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 92% year over year.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue reached $75.246 billion (+92% YoY) in Q1 FY27\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74, e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e124 [rumour; mlq.ai; original source Reuters, citing three unnamed sources with knowledge of the matter 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek is developing a custom AI inference chip, according to a Reuters report citing three unnamed sources, creating a potential competitor to NVIDIA GPUs in inference workloads.\n    Quote: DeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e129; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e125 [rumour; mlq.ai; original source Reuters, citing three unnamed sources with knowledge of the matter 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek has been expanding its chip engineering team and discussing its inference-chip project with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers; the project is described as early-stage.\n    Quote: The project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2]. The company is in discussions with chip design firms, semiconductor foundries, and memory suppliers, though no specific partners have been named.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130, e131; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e126 [fact; mlq.ai; original source DeepSeek ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek cut prices for its V4-Pro API by 75% in May 2026, adding to price competition among Chinese AI providers and potentially affecting demand for AI inference hardware.\n    Quote: In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5].\n- e127 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\n    Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e128 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Analyst Richard Windey ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windey said U.S. export rules have effectively eliminated NVIDIA's China revenue and also constrain DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced chips outside China.\n    Quote: Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3].\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e129 [rumour; idnfinancials.com; original source Unnamed Reuters sources familiar with DeepSeek's plans ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Reuters, citing three sources familiar with the plans, reported that DeepSeek is developing AI chips designed specifically for inference.\n    Quote: According to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e124, e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e130 [rumour; idnfinancials.com; original source Unnamed Reuters sources familiar with DeepSeek's plans ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Reuters sources said DeepSeek had communicated with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers over the past year to support its chip development.\n    Quote: Over the past year, the company has been communicating with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e131 [rumour; idnfinancials.com; original source Unnamed Reuters sources familiar with DeepSeek's plans ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Reuters sources said DeepSeek increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months as it developed its own chips.\n    Quote: DeepSeek has also increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e132 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source idnfinancials.com 2026-07-07; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] The article reported that Huawei controls around half of China's AI chip market, valued at approximately US$50 billion.\n    Quote: Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.\n- e133 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source idnfinancials.com 2026-07-07; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] The article reported a surge in demand for Huawei Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies following the DeepSeek V4-Flash launch.\n    Quote: The launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies.\n- e134 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source Richard Windsor, Radio Free Mobile ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windsor said Nvidia's position in China was effectively zero and would remain so, and that DeepSeek's chip development would not affect Nvidia.\n    Quote: \"Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way. DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology,\" said analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile, adding that the development would not affect the chipmaker.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e128; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e139 [rumour; thenextweb.com; original source NVIDIA and d-Matrix, as reported by The Information ; from gnews:c2ccca1a61b99adc685b1aa2 2026-07-08] The Information reported that NVIDIA and d-Matrix plan to ship a joint AI-model inference system, with Parasail as its first customer and the system due online later in 2026.\n    Quote: The GPU giant is combining its hardware with inference chips from the startup d-Matrix , The Information reported. The two will ship a joint system to run AI models. The AI-cloud firm Parasail will be its first customer, with the system due online later this year.\n- e140 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-08; from gnews:f7a5bf37167c4f05c3585a2f 2026-07-08] NVIDIA said GPU-accelerated Presto integrated with IBM watsonx.data is now ready for production-workload testing; no period or amount was stated.\n    Quote: With integration in the IBM watsonx.data platform, GPU-accelerated Presto is now ready for testing on your production workloads.\n- e141 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source gurufocus.com (analyst) 2026-07-08; from gnews:bbb38537c01d8be774edc057 2026-07-08] The author reported that demand for high-bandwidth memory chips accelerated in 2026, helping lead Micron's gains.\n    Quote: demand for high-bandwidth memory chips accelerated\n- e146 [third_party_view; futuriom.com, summarizing Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Meta is setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models, Bloomberg reported on July 1, 2026.\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models.\n- e149 [third_party_view; Futuriom; original source Futuriom 2026-07-08; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Futuriom said NVIDIA’s new AI-cloud business model could boost GPU demand from startups and other small enterprises.\n    Quote: Futuriom Take: NVIDIA’s new business model for AI cloud providers could boost demand for its GPUs among startups and other small enterprises.\n- e150 [fact; CNBC; original source SambaNova 2026-07-08; from gnews:6b89157155648b9ec317d209 2026-07-08] SambaNova raised $1 billion in financing, valuing it at $11 billion; the round was led by General Atlantic, with participation from Seligman Ventures, T. Rowe Price and Capital Group.\n    Quote: SambaNova is now valued at $11 billion thanks to the financing, which was led by General Atlantic along with participation from Seligman Ventures, T. Rowe Price and Capital Group.\n- e151 [fact; CNBC; original source JPMorgan Chase 2026-07-08; from gnews:6b89157155648b9ec317d209 2026-07-08] JPMorgan Chase said it would deploy SambaNova systems for on-prem inference in its enterprise AI workloads.\n    Quote: On Wednesday, JPMorgan Chase said it would deploy SambaNova's systems for \"on-prem inference in our demanding enterprise AI workloads.\"\n- e152 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] The author says NVIDIA committed money to train plumbers and electricians; the amount and timing are not specified.\n    Quote: Nvidia just committed serious money to train plumbers and electricians, and the retailer that sells to those exact tradespeople responded by printing a 52-week low.\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from 