{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '543,748p' poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md\"","aggregated_output":"### request for v9 on 2026-07-12\n```\nThesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 83 items]\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e104 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA and Nokia 2026-07-07; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to integrate Nokia anyRAN software with NVIDIA AI-RAN platforms for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\n- e105 [fact; NVIDIA; original source SoftBank and NVIDIA ; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] SoftBank and NVIDIA reported a trial of stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform, delivering roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\n    Quote: SoftBank and NVIDIA recently reported stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform. The trial delivered roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\n- e106 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Palantir Technologies ; from gnews:1a116fecd5de1747cc5a06b1 2026-07-07] Palantir announced it is combining its new intelligence engine with NVIDIA’s Nemotron open AI models to deliver mission-focused AI for U.S. government agencies and major infrastructure operators.\n    Quote: Earlier this week, Palantir announced that it is combining its new intelligence engine and Nvidia's Nemotron open AI models to deliver mission-focused AI for government agencies and major infrastructure operators in the U.S.\n- e108 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its profit-sharing plan reflects a shift in AI demand from model building toward production inference that requires infrastructure to run continuously at scale.\n    Quote: Nvidia said that this plan highlights a transition in AI demand from model building towards production inference tasks, needing continuous running of infrastructure at scale.\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA’s AI computing products.\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\n- e114 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier.\n    Quote: That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\n- e116 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\n    Quote: tight market conditions could last until 2028\n- e117 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author said new capacity for many companies in the memory-chip industry would not be online until 2027 or later.\n    Quote: new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later\n- e122 [fact; 247Wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 92% year over year.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue reached $75.246 billion (+92% YoY) in Q1 FY27\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74, e161, e239; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e124 [rumour; mlq.ai; original source Reuters, citing three unnamed sources with knowledge of the matter 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek is developing a custom AI inference chip, according to a Reuters report citing three unnamed sources, creating a potential competitor to NVIDIA GPUs in inference workloads.\n    Quote: DeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e129; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e125 [rumour; mlq.ai; original source Reuters, citing three unnamed sources with knowledge of the matter 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek has been expanding its chip engineering team and discussing its inference-chip project with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers; the project is described as early-stage.\n    Quote: The project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2]. The company is in discussions with chip design firms, semiconductor foundries, and memory suppliers, though no specific partners have been named.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130, e131; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e126 [fact; mlq.ai; original source DeepSeek ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek cut prices for its V4-Pro API by 75% in May 2026, adding to price competition among Chinese AI providers and potentially affecting demand for AI inference hardware.\n    Quote: In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5].\n- e127 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\n    Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e128 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Analyst Richard Windey ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windey said U.S. export rules have effectively eliminated NVIDIA's China revenue and also constrain DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced chips outside China.\n    Quote: Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3].\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e129 [rumour; idnfinancials.com; original source Unnamed Reuters sources familiar with DeepSeek's plans ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Reuters, citing three sources familiar with the plans, reported that DeepSeek is developing AI chips designed specifically for inference.\n    Quote: According to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e124, e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e130 [rumour; idnfinancials.com; original source Unnamed Reuters sources familiar with DeepSeek's plans ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Reuters sources said DeepSeek had communicated with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers over the past year to support its chip development.\n    Quote: Over the past year, the company has been communicating with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e131 [rumour; idnfinancials.com; original source Unnamed Reuters sources familiar with DeepSeek's plans ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Reuters sources said DeepSeek increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months as it developed its own chips.\n    Quote: DeepSeek has also increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e132 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source idnfinancials.com 2026-07-07; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] The article reported that Huawei controls around half of China's AI chip market, valued at approximately US$50 billion.\n    Quote: Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.\n- e133 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source idnfinancials.com 2026-07-07; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] The article reported a surge in demand for Huawei Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies following the DeepSeek V4-Flash launch.\n    Quote: The launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies.\n- e134 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source Richard Windsor, Radio Free Mobile ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windsor said Nvidia's position in China was effectively zero and would remain so, and that DeepSeek's chip development would not affect Nvidia.\n    Quote: \"Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way. DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology,\" said analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile, adding that the development would not affect the chipmaker.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e128; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e139 [rumour; thenextweb.com; original source NVIDIA and d-Matrix, as reported by The Information ; from gnews:c2ccca1a61b99adc685b1aa2 2026-07-08] The Information reported that NVIDIA and d-Matrix plan to ship a joint AI-model inference system, with Parasail as its first customer and the system due online later in 2026.\n    Quote: The GPU giant is combining its hardware with inference chips from the startup d-Matrix , The Information reported. The two will ship a joint system to run AI models. The AI-cloud firm Parasail will be its first customer, with the system due online later this year.\n- e140 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-08; from gnews:f7a5bf37167c4f05c3585a2f 2026-07-08] NVIDIA said GPU-accelerated Presto integrated with IBM watsonx.data is now ready for production-workload testing; no period or amount was stated.\n    Quote: With integration in the IBM watsonx.data platform, GPU-accelerated Presto is now ready for testing on your production workloads.\n- e141 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source gurufocus.com (analyst) 2026-07-08; from gnews:bbb38537c01d8be774edc057 2026-07-08] The author reported that demand for high-bandwidth memory chips accelerated in 2026, helping lead Micron's gains.\n    Quote: demand for high-bandwidth memory chips accelerated\n- e146 [third_party_view; futuriom.com, summarizing Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Meta is setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models, Bloomberg reported on July 1, 2026.\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models.\n- e149 [third_party_view; Futuriom; original source Futuriom 2026-07-08; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Futuriom said NVIDIA’s new AI-cloud business model could boost GPU demand from startups and other small enterprises.\n    Quote: Futuriom Take: NVIDIA’s new business model for AI cloud providers could boost demand for its GPUs among startups and other small enterprises.\n- e150 [fact; CNBC; original source SambaNova 2026-07-08; from gnews:6b89157155648b9ec317d209 2026-07-08] SambaNova raised $1 billion in financing, valuing it at $11 billion; the round was led by General Atlantic, with participation from Seligman Ventures, T. Rowe Price and Capital Group.\n    Quote: SambaNova is now valued at $11 billion thanks to the financing, which was led by General Atlantic along with participation from Seligman Ventures, T. Rowe Price and Capital Group.\n- e151 [fact; CNBC; original source JPMorgan Chase 2026-07-08; from gnews:6b89157155648b9ec317d209 2026-07-08] JPMorgan Chase said it would deploy SambaNova systems for on-prem inference in its enterprise AI workloads.\n    Quote: On Wednesday, JPMorgan Chase said it would deploy SambaNova's systems for \"on-prem inference in our demanding enterprise AI workloads.\"\n- e152 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] The author says NVIDIA committed money to train plumbers and electricians; the amount and timing are not specified.\n    Quote: Nvidia just committed serious money to train plumbers and electricians, and the retailer that sells to those exact tradespeople responded by printing a 52-week low.\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e154 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] 80% of Chinese executives surveyed said their total infrastructure spending is running over budget in 2026, mostly because AI-related projects are costly.\n    Quote: In addition, 80% of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\n- e155 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, quoting the Bloomberg Intelligence report; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence report, based on a survey of 60 executives, said China’s shift from foreign to domestic AI semiconductors is progressing and is likely to benefit Huawei and Hygon.\n    Quote: “China’s drive to substitute locally made AI-semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic markers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\n- e156 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey expects Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local suppliers gain share.\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\n- e157 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] China is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) over five years to build data centers nationwide as part of a government-led AI expansion initiative.\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) to build data centers across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\n- e158 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies will supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China’s data-center initiative.\n    Quote: At least 80% of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\n- e159 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the survey report; original source Bloomberg Intelligence report 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The report said a global memory-chip shortage is likely to constrain Chinese AI firms’ growth, with high-bandwidth memory supply emerging as a bottleneck and ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit.\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. with ChangXin Memory Technologies Inc. poised to reap the benefits. The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report.\n- e160 [third_party_view; theregister.com; original source Artificial Analysis ; from gnews:b2ff1fae25f82fd3111134de 2026-07-08] Artificial Analysis measured SambaNova's platform using four NVIDIA H200 GPUs and 16 SN50 RDUs at 763 tokens per second on MiniMax M2.7 at a 10,000-token context length.\n    Quote: At short context lengths (10,000 tokens) Artificial Analysis found SambaNova's heterogeneous compute platform, which combined four H200 GPUs with 16 of its SN50 RDUs was able to achieve decode speed of 763 tok/s in MiniMax M2.7.\n- e161 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ee0d6792b6bf98069cfe0cc6 2026-07-08] NVIDIA's Data Center segment reported record revenue of $75.2 billion in fiscal Q1 2027.\n    Quote: its data center segment set a record at $75.2 billion\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e201, e204, e209; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e162 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America reported that NVIDIA’s sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year over year, nearly twice the growth in cloud capital expenditures.\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia’s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\n- e163 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America modeled NVIDIA retaining roughly 65% to 70% or more of AI capital expenditures over time, with the remainder going to ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\n    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\n- e165 [fact; PR Newswire company release; original source Parasail and d-Matrix 2026-07-08; from gnews:040c256543fc8106ffecabae 2026-07-08] Parasail announced it is deploying d-Matrix Corsair accelerators alongside its NVIDIA Hopper and Blackwell infrastructure for inference services; the announcement claims up to 10x faster, more cost-efficient inference.\n    Quote: Parasail, the inference cloud for AI-native startups, and d-Matrix, a pioneer in low-latency AI inference compute platforms for data centers, today announced Parasail is deploying d-Matrix Corsair inference accelerators alongside the NVIDIA Hopper and NVIDIA Blackwell architectures to deliver up to 10x faster, more cost-efficient inference services to its customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e139 (); both are in force: The Information reported that NVIDIA and d-Matrix plan to ship a joint AI-model inference system, with Parasail as its first customer and the system due online later in 2026.\n- e166 [rumour; barchart.com; original source barchart.com ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Reports say DeepSeek is developing an inference-focused AI chip that could reduce its reliance on Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e129; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e169 [fact; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA reported record Data Center segment revenue in its first quarter; the amount is not stated.\n    Quote: Revenues from Nvidia's data center segment -- the primary engine of the business -- set a record in the first quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e170 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\n    Quote: management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter.\n- e171 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n- e175 [third_party_view; QZ; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Bloomberg Intelligence projected that Nvidia would hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\n    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence projects the company will hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\n- e176 [third_party_view; QZ; original source QZ 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Amazon could launch its Trainium4 AI chip later in 2026.\n    Quote: Amazon 's next chip, Trainium4, which packs three times the performance of its predecessor, could arrive later this year.\n- e177 [party_statement; QZ; original source Andy Jassy ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said Trainium2 had no spare capacity left to sell in late 2025.\n    Quote: Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in late 2025 that Trainium2, the previous generation, had no spare capacity left to sell.\n- e178 [party_statement; QZ; original source Andy Jassy ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Andy Jassy said Amazon's Trainium2 business was generating multibillion-dollar revenue and growing at a 150% quarterly rate in late 2025.\n    Quote: He said it had grown into a multibillion-dollar business at a 150% quarterly clip.\n- e179 [fact; QZ; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Microsoft announced Maia 200 in January 2026, and the chip was reaching a handful of U.S. data centers by July 2026.\n    Quote: It announced Maia 200, a chip built for that work, in January 2026, and the chip is already reaching a handful of U.S. data centers.\n- e180 [fact; QZ; original source Meta ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 300 chip was in production for ranking and recommendation workloads by July 2026.\n    Quote: The newest version, MTIA 300, is already in production for ranking and recommendation work.\n- e181 [party_statement; QZ; original source Meta ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta planned to build three more MTIA chip generations over the following two years for generative AI workloads.\n    Quote: Meta is set to build three more generations over the next two years with the purpose of running generative AI models.\n- e182 [third_party_view; QZ; original source QZ 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 450 was expected to arrive in early 2027.\n    Quote: The next one, MTIA 450, arrives in early 2027, doubling how fast the chip moves data in and out of memory.\n- e183 [third_party_view; QZ; original source QZ 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 500 was expected to arrive later in 2027.\n    Quote: Its eventual successor, MTIA 500, is expected to come later next year, and pushes memory speed another 50% higher.\n- e184 [party_statement; QZ; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] OpenAI planned to begin installing its custom AI chips in the second half of 2026 and continue through the end of 2029.\n    Quote: Installing them is set to begin in the second half of 2026 and run through the end of 2029.\n- e185 [fact; QZ; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla completed the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026.\n    Quote: Tesla finished the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026, according to Reuters.\n- e186 [party_statement; QZ; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla planned to produce its AI5 chip at TSMC's Arizona plant and Samsung's Texas plant.\n    Quote: Production is planned at both TSMC 's plant in Arizona and Samsung's plant in Texas.\n- e187 [party_statement; QZ; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla targeted mid-2027 for mass production of its AI5 chip.\n    Quote: Tesla is targeting mid-2027 for mass production.\n- e188 [fact; QZ; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] SpaceX filed plans in May 2026 for a Texas chip plant called Terafab with a stated cost of $55 billion and possible total investment of up to $119 billion.\n    Quote: SpaceX, Musk's other property, filed plans in May for a $55 billion chip manufacturing plant in Texas called Terafab, Reuters reported . Total investment in the plant could reach $119 billion.\n- e189 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta 2026-07-09; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\n- e190 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Quanta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Quanta's monthly sales rose to NT$385.19 billion in June 2026, more than doubling year over year, according to Bloomberg data compiled for the item.\n    Quote: Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n- e191 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Hon Hai Precision Industry ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Hon Hai's quarterly sales rose 40% year over year, exceeding expectations, according to Bloomberg.\n    Quote: allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\n- e196 [fact; AWS; original source AWS 2026-07-10; from gnews:23d831f15e3a80d6b3ba60e6 2026-07-10] Amazon SageMaker AI introduced serverless customization for NVIDIA’s Nemotron 3 Nano and Super models.\n    Quote: Now, SageMaker AI introduces serverless model customization for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 models, starting with Nemotron 3 Nano (30B total parameters, 3B active) and Nemotron 3 Super (120B total parameters, 12B active).\n- e197 [fact; Trefis.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] NVIDIA revenue grew 71% over the last twelve months.\n    Quote: revenue grew 71% over the last twelve months\n- e198 [fact; Trefis.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] NVIDIA revenue grew 85% in the most recent quarter.\n    Quote: 85% in the most recent quarter.\n- e199 [party_statement; Trefis.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] NVIDIA management called the Blackwell architecture ramp the fastest product ramp in the company’s history.\n    Quote: the company called the “fastest product ramp in our company’s history,”\n- e201 [fact; 247wallst.com, reporting NVIDIA's figures; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year; networking revenue grew 199%.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year, and networking alone grew 199%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161, e205, e210; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e202 [party_statement; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA guided to total revenue of $91 billion for Q2 FY2027.\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance points to $91 billion in revenue.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e204 [fact; David Moadel, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, including Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion.\n    Quote: The company reported record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with Data Center revenue reaching $75.2 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161, e254; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e205 [fact; David Moadel, citing NVIDIA’s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA Data Center Networking revenue was $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\n    Quote: Within that segment, Data Center Networking alone hit $14.8 billion, up 199% from a year earlier, reflecting surging demand for the Spectrum-X Ethernet and NVLink interconnect systems that travel with every rack-scale Blackwell deployment.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e209 [fact; 247Wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e210 [fact; TIKR author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75 billion, up 92% year over year; the quarter is not specified.\n    Quote: Data center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201, e249; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e227 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI starting late 2026 and continuing into 2027.\n    Quote: However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\n- e239 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion, up 92% year over year.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue climbed 92% YOY to $75.2 billion, highlighting that AI infrastructure remains the company's primary growth engine.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e242 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported quarterly revenue of about $35 billion, up 198% year over year and a record.\n    Quote: In the recent quarter, for example, the company reported revenue growth of 198% to about $35 billion dollars, a record level.\n- e243 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported that quarterly net income rose 398% year over year.\n    Quote: Net income soared 398%\n- e244 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported a 72% operating margin in the recent quarter.\n    Quote: operating margin came in at 72%.\n- e245 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Counterpoint Research ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] Counterpoint Research estimated SK Hynix held 58% of the global HBM market at the end of the first quarter.\n    Quote: SK Hynix held 58% share globally of the HBM market as of the end of the first quarter, according to Counterpoint Research.\n- e246 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix and NVIDIA ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix and NVIDIA signed a multi-year technology partnership under which SK Hynix will supply memory for areas including AI infrastructure, personal AI, and robotics.\n    Quote: The two companies even reinforced their relationship recently, signing a multi-year technology partnership. It involves SK Hynix supplying memory across a broad range of areas, from AI infrastructure to personal AI and robotics .\n- e247 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted by fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said SK Hynix has been NVIDIA's largest memory partner and will continue to be its largest memory partner.\n    Quote: \"SK Hynix has been Nvidia's largest memory partner and will continue to be our largest memory partner,\" Nvidia chief Jensen Huang said at the time.\n- e249 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:635f19578778394086009f6f 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75.2 billion in its fiscal quarter ended in late April 2026, up 92% year over year.\n    Quote: Data center revenue of $75.2 billion climbed 92%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e210; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e254 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported record FY2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\n    Quote: During the fiscal first quarter of 2027, Nvidia generated record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year-over-year (YOY).\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e255 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\n    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Themes in force, 3]\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The reference is the current status of no completed authorization, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus or company guidance on authorization by the deadline is supplied. The specific reports that China plans to permit selected firms to buy H200s, together with the report that officials are work\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). The evidence still implies about $650 billion: the 2026 estimates and NVIDIA management's cited projection point to that level, while the power-constraint view gives no quantified reducti\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-12; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies spending around, and modestly above, $1 trillion: it does not quantify a move to the $1.1 trillion boundary, while the stated power constraint is downside pressure withou\n\n\n```\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md | sed -n '1,87p;640,658p' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md | sed -n '1,87p;640,658p' \"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Readers' packet: six sequences of two questions, NVIDIA, 2026-07-10 to 2026-07-12\n     2\t\n     3\tv9: \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"; v14: \"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report\n     4\tfor FY2027?\". Each sequence judged both questions each day it was due, from the same starting state. Apply `reading-rule.md`\n     5\t(A to D and the pass line) to each sequence. The material both questions were shown each day is at the end.\n     6\t\n     7\t## sequence-K\n     8\t- first-attempt refusals: none\n     9\t### v14 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n    10\t- reference: Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection: $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n    11\t- implied outcome: $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's projection; no separate quantified evidence in view supports adjusting it up or down.\n    12\t- reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. The supplied evidence gives no separate quantified revision to that projection: $393 billion is $7 billion below rung 2's $400 billion threshold and $43 billion above rung 4's $350 billion threshold, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\n    13\t- cites: [\"e195\"]; cites_views: []\n    14\t- why changed: This is the first judgement.\n    15\t\n    16\t### v9 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n    17\t- reference: No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied. Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-sales projection (via v14), translated using the latest reported 92% Data Center share, gives a market-reference proxy of about $362 billion, in rung 3.\n    18\t- implied outcome: About $362 billion for FY2027: $393 billion of projected total revenue multiplied by the latest reported Data Center share of 92%. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold and above the $350 billion rung-3 threshold.\n    19\t- reasons: There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance. The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share: about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The latest quarter's $75.25 billion of Data Center revenue was up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially; Q2 total-revenue guidance is $91 billion and management says Data Center revenue growth will accelerate. Those support continued growth, but do not by themselves quantify a higher full-year segment forecast. Management's roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin visibility spans two calendar years and products, so it is not treated as FY2027 Data Center revenue in full. Competition and memory-supply constraints temper the upside. The implied $362 billion is below the $400 billion boundary for rung 2 and above the $350 billion boundary for rung 3, and it remains below the $393 billion total-revenue forecast.\n    20\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e109\", \"e116\", \"e117\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e163\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n    21\t- why changed: The rung changes from 2 to 3. The prior $420 billion estimate extrapolated Q1 sequential growth; this judgment instead anchors to the current $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection and applies the reported 92% Data Center mix, implying about $362 billion.\n    22\t\n    23\t### v14 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n    24\t- reference: Bloomberg’s FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n    25\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg’s stated projection; the separate 20% AI-laboratory demand figure has no period or revenue amount from which to derive a revision.\n    26\t- reasons: Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 projection is in rung 3, $7 billion below rung 2’s $400 billion threshold. The newer Morgan Stanley demand-mix figure adds no period or revenue amount, so it does not quantify a revision across that boundary. The bullish capex and China-authorization themes are consistent with upside risk, but do not size an increase enough to move the forecast from Bloomberg’s projection.\n    27\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\"]; cites_views: []\n    28\t- why changed: The rung and implied revenue remain unchanged, but the newly supplied Morgan Stanley demand-mix estimate adds a relevant supporting fact: AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of NVIDIA demand. It has no period or revenue quantification, so it does not change the implied FY2027 figure.\n    29\t\n    30\t### v9 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n    31\t- reference: Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, translated at the latest reported 92% Data Center share to about $362 billion; this sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\n    32\t- implied outcome: About $356 billion: Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion; applying the 92% latest segment share to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide implies about $83.7 billion for Q2. Carrying forward the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by the Q2 total-revenue guide gives about $93.3 billion in Q3 and $104 billion in Q4, for about $356 billion in FY2027.\n    33\t- reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The current run-rate estimate is about $356 billion, also rung 3: the implied quarterly progression does not reach rung 2's $400 billion floor. Q1's reported result and Q2 guidance support a strong trajectory, while the stated acceleration and multi-year visibility add upside but do not quantify enough FY2027 segment revenue to move the forecast across a boundary. Competition and memory constraints temper that upside. The implied segment figure remains below the dependent thesis's $393 billion total-revenue forecast.\n    34\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e202\", \"e109\", \"e116\", \"e153\", \"e159\", \"e162\", \"e163\", \"e165\", \"e177\", \"e179\", \"e180\", \"e184\", \"e187\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n    35\t- why changed: The rung is unchanged, but the implied figure changes from the previous estimate of about $362 billion to about $356 billion after incorporating the Q1 actual, Q2 total-revenue guidance and a forward run-rate calculation.\n    36\t\n    37\t### v14 on 2026-07-12: rung 2, changed True\n    38\t- reference: The most specific market reference supplied is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The broader Wall Street reference is only described as “well over $300 billion.”\n    39\t- implied outcome: About $410 billion: Bloomberg's $393 billion projection at 82% growth implies a FY2026 base of roughly $216 billion; carrying forward roughly 90% growth, informed by 85% reported Q1 growth and the Q2 guidance/96% growth expectation, implies approximately $410 billion.\n    40\t- reasons: The best quantified market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. But the reported 85% Q1 growth and management's $91 billion Q2 revenue guidance, alongside analysts' 96% Q2 growth expectation, point to a higher growth run-rate than Bloomberg's 82% full-year projection. Carrying roughly 90% growth forward puts the estimate around $410 billion, above rung 2's $400 billion boundary. China-related headwinds and reported supply constraints temper the upside but do not outweigh that run-rate evidence; the bullish capex and China-authorization themes are also consistent with the estimate.\n    41\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e238\", \"e240\", \"e224\", \"e241\", \"e231\", \"e232\", \"e252\", \"e253\"]; cites_views: []\n    42\t- why changed: The forecast moves from the prior $393 billion-based rung 3 assessment to an implied outcome around $410 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance provide new run-rate evidence beyond the prior quantified projection.\n    43\t\n    44\t### v9 on 2026-07-12: rung 3, changed True\n    45\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, converted using the latest roughly 92.2% Data Center share, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue: rung 3.\n    46\t- implied outcome: Approximately $378 billion: the current v14 forecast of about $410 billion in FY2027 total revenue multiplied by the latest Data Center share of about 92.2% ($75.25 billion of $81.6 billion). Q1's reported segment revenue and growth, $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guidance, and management's expectation of accelerating Data Center growth support that elevated run-rate.\n    47\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at NVIDIA's latest Data Center share of about 92.2% ($75.25 billion of $81.6 billion) to roughly $362 billion; that is rung 3. The new Q1 result, $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guidance and management's statement that Data Center growth will accelerate support a higher estimate. Mapping the current FY2027 total-revenue dependency forecast of about $410 billion at the latest segment mix implies approximately $378 billion of Data Center revenue. That is above rung 3's $350 billion floor but remains below rung 2's $400 billion floor; it is also below the dependent total-revenue forecast, as required. Multi-year processor visibility and strong hyperscaler demand support the estimate, while competition and memory constraints temper it.\n    48\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e255\", \"e109\", \"e116\", \"e153\", \"e158\", \"e162\", \"e163\", \"e227\"]; cites_views: [\"v14\"]\n    49\t- why changed: The rung remains 3, but the implied outcome has increased from about $356 billion to about $378 billion. The reported $75.25 billion Q1 Data Center result, Q2 guidance and acceleration commentary are now in force, and the current v14 total-revenue forecast is about $410 billion; the revised estimate still does not reach the $400 billion boundary.\n    50\t\n    51\t## sequence-L\n    52\t- first-attempt refusals: none\n    53\t### v14 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n    54\t- reference: Bloomberg's projected FY2027 sales of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n    55\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's direct full-year projection (reported as up 82%); that figure is below the $400 billion threshold for rung 2.\n    56\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. That same quantified FY2027 estimate supports rung 3; the themes in force all push demand and revenue upward, but none supplies a quantified increment that would move the implied outcome across the $400 billion boundary.\n    57\t- cites: [\"e195\"]; cites_views: []\n    58\t- why changed: This is the first judgement.\n    59\t\n    60\t### v9 on 2026-07-10: rung 3, changed True\n    61\t- reference: No direct FY2027 Data Center revenue consensus or NVIDIA FY2027 segment guidance is supplied, so a market-reference rung cannot be assigned. The closest company reference is Q2 FY2027 total-revenue guidance of $91 billion; it is not itself a rung-level FY2027 Data Center reference.\n    62\t- implied outcome: Approximately $357 billion for FY2027: estimate Q2 Data Center revenue at about $84 billion by applying Q1's roughly 92% Data Center share to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide, then carry forward the guide-implied roughly 11.5% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4 at the same mix; add those quarters to Q1's reported $75.25 billion.\n    63\t- reasons: No direct FY2027 Data Center revenue consensus or segment guidance is supplied, so there is no FY2027 reference rung to anchor to. The nearest company guidance is $91 billion of total revenue for Q2 FY2027; it is a quarterly total, not a FY2027 Data Center estimate. As a rough forecast, applying the Q1 Data Center share (about 92%) to that Q2 total gives about $84 billion for Q2. Carrying forward the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by the $91 billion total-revenue guidance, with that mix held constant for Q3 and Q4, implies approximately $357 billion for FY2027. This is within rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Q1's 21% sequential Data Center growth, management's statement that Q2 Data Center growth would accelerate, and broad revenue visibility support upside, but the numerical Q2 total-revenue guide constrains the near-term run-rate; the $1 trillion visibility spans two calendar years and multiple products, so it is not taken as FY2027 Data Center revenue. China substitution, competition, and memory tightness are downside pressures, while strong hyperscaler demand and the capex themes support the forecast. The outcome is a forecast, not an established FY2027 result.\n    64\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e202\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e205\", \"e116\", \"e109\", \"e153\", \"e158\", \"e162\", \"e163\"]; cites_views: []\n    65\t- why changed: The rung moves down from 2 to 3. The new $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guidance constrains the near-term run-rate; using it with Q1's Data Center mix and carrying its implied sequential growth forward produces an estimate of about $357 billion, rather than the prior estimate of about $420 billion. The updated implied outcome and supporting calculation change the conclusion.\n    66\t\n    67\t### v14 on 2026-07-11: rung 3, changed True\n    68\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n    69\t- implied outcome: About $393 billion: use Bloomberg's quantified FY2027 projection as the baseline; Morgan Stanley's roughly 20% AI-laboratory demand share provides no period-specific revenue amount to add. The implied figure remains below $400 billion.\n    70\t- reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies rung 3: the new demand-mix information points upward but does not quantify an FY2027 revenue increment sufficient to cross the $400 billion boundary.\n    71\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e213\"]; cites_views: []\n    72\t- why changed: The rung and implied figure remain unchanged, but e213 adds a new quantified fact about AI laboratories' share of NVIDIA demand. It is a qualitative upward signal for FY2027 revenue, not a quantified revision to the Bloomberg estimate.\n    73\t\n    74\t### v14 on 2026-07-12: rung 2, changed True\n    75\t- reference: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the specific market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street's reported expectation of “well over $300 billion” is less precise.\n    76\t- implied outcome: About $412 billion: Bloomberg's $393 billion projection at 82% growth implies an FY2026 base of about $216 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion at 85% growth and the $91 billion Q2 guide (about 96% growth per analyst expectations) imply roughly 91% first-half growth; carrying that rate onto the inferred FY2026 second-half base gives an FY2027 total near $412 billion.\n    77\t- reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, in rung 3. The new Q1 result and Q2 guidance lift the implied FY2027 run-rate above that reference: carrying the roughly 91% first-half growth rate into the second half implies about $412 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. Competitive alternatives and memory constraints are downside pressures, but the supplied evidence does not quantify a reduction large enough to pull the forecast back below $400 billion.\n    78\t- cites: [\"e195\", \"e238\", \"e240\", \"e224\", \"e220\", \"e221\", \"e222\", \"e231\", \"e232\", \"e234\", \"e252\", \"e253\", \"e257\", \"e241\"]; cites_views: []\n    79\t- why changed: The previous rung relied on Bloomberg's $393 billion projection and lacked a quantified increment to cross $400 billion. The newly supplied Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance support an implied estimate of about $412 billion, changing the rung.\n    80\t\n    81\t### v9 on 2026-07-12: rung 2, changed True\n    82\t- reference: No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied. The nearest company reference is Q2 FY2027 total revenue guidance of approximately $91 billion; using Q1's roughly 92% Data Center mix and carrying the guidance-implied sequential growth forward gives about $357 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3.\n    83\t- implied outcome: Approximately $405 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. The Q1 result and Q2 total-revenue guidance imply a run-rate near $357 billion when the guided sequential growth is carried into the remaining quarters; the $1 trillion two-calendar-year Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility is discounted rather than taken in full, but raises the forecast across the $400 billion rung boundary.\n    84\t- reasons: There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance. The closest company reference is Q2 FY2027 total-revenue guidance of about $91 billion; applying Q1's roughly 92% Data Center mix gives about $84 billion for Q2. Carrying the guidance-implied roughly 11.5% sequential growth rate forward for Q3 and Q4 gives an annual reference of about $357 billion, in rung 3. The evidence now supports a higher forecast: management said Q2 Data Center growth would accelerate and cited roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026 and 2027. That visibility is not a FY2027 segment forecast and is discounted for its two-calendar-year span and execution, but, alongside the strong reported run-rate, it lifts the implied outcome to around $405 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. China's domestic substitution and memory-supply constraints temper the upside, but do not outweigh the demand and visibility evidence.\n    85\t- cites: [\"e74\", \"e170\", \"e171\", \"e255\", \"e153\", \"e156\", \"e158\", \"e159\", \"e116\", \"e117\"]; cites_views: []\n    86\t- why changed: The implied FY2027 estimate has moved up from the previous conclusion's roughly $357 billion to around $405 billion, crossing from rung 3 to rung 2. The key new quantified support is management's roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility across 2026 and 2027; Q2 acceleration guidance adds support but is not quantified.\n    87\t\n   640\t    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\n   641\t- e165 [fact; PR Newswire company release; original source Parasail and d-Matrix 2026-07-08; from gnews:040c256543fc8106ffecabae 2026-07-08] Parasail announced it is deploying d-Matrix Corsair accelerators alongside its NVIDIA Hopper and Blackwell infrastructure for inference services; the announcement claims up to 10x faster, more cost-efficient inference.\n   642\t    Quote: Parasail, the inference cloud for AI-native startups, and d-Matrix, a pioneer in low-latency AI inference compute platforms for data centers, today announced Parasail is deploying d-Matrix Corsair inference accelerators alongside the NVIDIA Hopper and NVIDIA Blackwell architectures to deliver up to 10x faster, more cost-efficient inference services to its customers.\n   643\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e139 (); both are in force: The Information reported that NVIDIA and d-Matrix plan to ship a joint AI-model inference system, with Parasail as its first customer and the system due online later in 2026.\n   644\t- e166 [rumour; barchart.com; original source barchart.com ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Reports say DeepSeek is developing an inference-focused AI chip that could reduce its reliance on Nvidia GPUs.\n   645\t    Quote: Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their AI infrastructure.\n   646\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e129; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   647\t- e169 [fact; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA reported record Data Center segment revenue in its first quarter; the amount is not stated.\n   648\t    Quote: Revenues from Nvidia's data center segment -- the primary engine of the business -- set a record in the first quarter.\n   649\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   650\t- e170 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\n   651\t    Quote: management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter.\n   652\t- e171 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n   653\t    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n   654\t- e175 [third_party_view; QZ; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Bloomberg Intelligence projected that Nvidia would hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\n   655\t    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence projects the company will hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\n   656\t- e176 [third_party_view; QZ; original source QZ 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Amazon could launch its Trainium4 AI chip later in 2026.\n   657\t    Quote: Amazon 's next chip, Trainium4, which packs three times the performance of its predecessor, could arrive later this year.\n   658\t- e177 [party_statement; QZ; original source Andy Jassy ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said Trainium2 had no spare capacity left to sell in late 2025.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
