{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"**Blind verdict: the answers meet the pass thresholds. The full pass remains unverified because the packet does not show the link commits at sequence 148.** K and N used the company forecast to calculate Data Center revenue. M calculated Data Center revenue independently and used the company forecast as a cross-check.\n\nI counted **K, M and N as linked** because their v9 answers cite and discuss v14. I counted **L, P and Q as controls** because their v9 answers never cite v14. [Reader packet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:7)\n\n## July 12 scores\n\nAll figures are in USD billions. For C, the Q1 actuals leave **$81.6B − $75.25B = $6.35B** for the rest of the company. The frozen rule sets the annual minimum at **$6B**. [Frozen reading rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:5)\n\n| Sequence | A · FY2027 Data Center / total | B · Arithmetic and sources | C · Room for the rest | D · Use of the link |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| K | Pass · ~$378B / ~$410B | Both pass | Pass · $32B | Use shown · commit unverified |\n| L | Pass · ~$405B / ~$412B | v9 fails · v14 passes | Pass · $7B | N/A |\n| M | Pass · ~$356B / ~$400B | v9 passes · v14 fails | Pass · $44B | Fail · cross-check only |\n| N | Pass · ~$360B / ~$387B | Both pass | Pass · $27B | Use shown · commit unverified |\n| P | Pass · $390–395B / ~$411B | v9 fails · v14 passes | Pass · $16B at the high end | N/A |\n| Q | Pass · ~$370B / ~$411B | v9 fails · v14 passes | Pass · $41B | N/A |\n\n- **K.** For v14, $393B ÷ 1.82 = $215.93B, and $215.93B × 1.90 = $410.27B. The roughly 90% growth assumption is stated and supported by Q1’s reported 85% and analysts’ Q2 expectation of 96%. For v9, $75.25B ÷ $81.6B = 92.22%. Applying that share to v14’s $410B gives $378.09B. Applying it to Bloomberg’s $393B gives $362.42B, which the answer keeps as a separate reference. The Q1 figures are printed together in v9 and satisfy C. Neither answer supplies a Q2 segment forecast. [K answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:37)\n- **L.** For v14, the inferred prior-year first half is $81.6B ÷ 1.85 + $91B ÷ 1.96 = $90.54B. The current first half is $172.6B, up 90.64%. Carrying that rate onto the inferred prior-year second half of $125.40B gives $411.66B for the year. For v9, the Q2 guide and roughly 92% mix give an ~$84B Q2 baseline against $91B total. Carrying forward the guide-implied growth gives ~$356–357B for the year, depending on how the share is rounded. The answer gives no allocation or discount that explains the further ~$48B to $405B. The $1 trillion visibility supports the direction of the revision, but its two-calendar-year product scope leaves the FY2027 segment amount unsized. [L answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:74)\n- **M.** For v9, $91B × 92% = $83.72B in Q2. Carrying forward 11.52% sequential growth gives ~$93.36B and ~$104.12B for Q3 and Q4. With Q1’s $75.25B, that is ~$356.45B. The Q2 proxy fits under the $91B total guide. For v14, carrying forward Q1’s 85% alone gives $393B ÷ 1.82 × 1.85 = $399.48B. That explains the neighborhood of $400B, but the answer leaves Q2’s contribution and second-half moderation unsized while claiming it crosses $400B. I used the stated ~$400B for A and C and failed B on the unsupported crossing. V9’s ~$356B ÷ ~$400B = 89% is an after-the-fact comparison. The answer explicitly derives its forecast independently, which fails D. [M answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:118)\n- **N.** For v14, $91B ÷ $81.6B − 1 = 11.52%. Carrying that forward gives $101.48B and $113.17B for Q3 and Q4, for $387.26B total. For v9, $387B × 92% = $356.04B. I accepted ~$360B for B because the stated acceleration supports a small upward allowance and $356.04B already rounds to $360B at the precision used. I used the final ~$360B for C. V14 supplies quarterly amounts, but v9 gives no comparable July 12 segment dollar amounts by quarter. [N answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:162)\n- **P.** For v14, the average of 85% and 96% is 90.5%, and $393B ÷ 1.82 × 1.905 = $411.35B. For v9, $91B × 92% = $83.72B, so the ~$84B Q2 proxy fits under the total guide. The acceleration allowance is unsized. After $75.25B in Q1 and the ~$84B starting point for Q2, reaching $390–395B requires roughly $231–236B in Q3 and Q4 before any Q2 uplift. The answer gives no growth rate or allocation that gets there. The hypothetical $1 trillion ÷ 2 = $500B per calendar year is arithmetically correct, but assigning it a FY2027 Data Center rung also fails B’s scope check. [P answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:199)\n- **Q.** V14 uses the same disclosed 90.5% calculation as P and gets ~$411B. V9’s 92% mix and 11.52% sequential carry-forward reproduce the ~$356B baseline. The implied Q2 proxy is ~$83.72B against the $91B guide. I failed B on the ~$14B uplift to $370B. Acceleration, processor visibility and hyperscaler sales support the direction, but the answer gives no basis for the size of the adjustment, which exceeds the rounding used for the baseline. [Q answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:236)\n\n## Bloomberg’s $393B and the link\n\nBloomberg’s compiled $393B company forecast, e195, is visible in v14’s source material. It also appears in **all nine linked v9 answers** across the window. That shows the figure reached those answers. The packet does not establish whether the e195 source record itself was delivered to linked v9. On July 12, K, M and N all use $393B to calculate a separate ~$362B segment proxy. K sizes its final forecast from v14’s **$410B**, and N from v14’s **$387B**. M’s final **$356B** comes from its own quarterly calculation. [Linked answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:7) · [Bloomberg source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:473)\n\n## Harm and pass line\n\n- **First-attempt refusals: zero.** All six sequences report none. The packet has 18 v14 and 15 v9 answers. L, P and Q have no July 11 v9 answer shown, and their refusal summaries record none. [Reader packet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:1)\n- **New material grounding errors: zero observed across the 33 answers shown.** The linked answers preserve Bloomberg’s $393B as a company projection and identify the segment figures as derived estimates. K and N describe the revised v14 figures as system forecasts. The closest call was P’s July 10 and July 12 use of a Data Center rung for the hypothetical $500B annual split. I counted that as a scope problem. I did not count it as material grounding harm because both answers explicitly explain the split’s broader product and period scope and give a separate Data Center forecast. The unsupported uplifts and M’s unsupported threshold crossing are B misses. [P’s July 10 answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:185) · [P’s July 12 answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/reader-packet.md:206)\n- **Pass thresholds.** K and N give **2 of 3** linked sequences that meet A–C and visibly use v14 as D requires. **0 of 3** controls meet A–C. All three sequences in each group are comparable, no ceiling stop is shown, and neither harm check triggers failure. **The full result is a Pass if K and N’s matching selections and commits at sequence 148 are confirmed.** The blind packet verifies their use of the forecasts and leaves that required record check open. [Frozen pass line](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:13)"}}
